\section{Results}
Both methods are about 95\% accurate (Table~\ref{tab:main}). The paired
difference is \DiffAcc{} \DiffCI{} (corrected $t=\DiffT$, $p=\DiffP$); LR wins
\WinsLR{} splits, $k$-NN \WinsKNN, \Ties{} are ties (Figure~\ref{fig:main}b).
The interval includes zero; differences of a few percentage points either way
cannot be ruled out. The narrower naive fold-level interval \DiffNaiveCI{} and
$t$ interval over the \NReps{} repetition means \DiffRepCI{} also include zero. Inner CV picks $k$ from
\KLo{} to \KHi{} (median \KMedian; $k=\KMode$ in \KModeCount{} of
\NFoldsTotal{} splits). In robustness runs (unscaled features, LR with $C$
tuned by inner CV, all $k$ from 1 to 30, \SensNSeeds{} other outer seeds) the
difference lies between \SensDiffMin{} and \SensDiffMax{} and
\SensVerdict. The Ibex and local runs \IdentityVerdict.

\paragraph{Limitations.} We claim neither method is better. Iris is small and
nearly separable, so this says little about other data; the corrected interval
is an approximation \citep{nadeau2003}; identity is shown for this
configuration only (Python \IbexPython{} vs.\ \PythonVersion, same pinned
packages, single-threaded BLAS).

